## Findings

### High — Phase 1 does not provide self-contained verification for its core live claims

The verify section defines isolated launch snippets and lists several `herdr pane` inputs, but it never supplies an executable sequence that creates the tab/panes, captures their returned IDs, invokes the inspector checkpoint before and after every required event, waits for convergence, records the timestamped ANSI UI/process evidence, or closes only those returned IDs. The listed commands also do not verify reload process continuity, a non-duplicated Pi lifecycle, normal host return, or UI state restoration; those checks exist only as prose. This falls short of the dispatch's requirement for self-contained verify commands and leaves most Phase 1 acceptance criteria unverifiable from the proposed command evidence. Add a concrete fail-closed operator/harness sequence with explicit assertions or evidence captures for every launch, action, reload, exit, and cleanup boundary.

### High — The proposed `lsof` evidence cannot prove client preservation or reload lifecycle behavior

`lsof -n -a -p "$daemon_pid" "$socket"` is filtered to PID `45621`, so `baseline_lsof`/`final_lsof` inventory only the daemon's own descriptor for the socket. It cannot show unrelated client attachments, detect an accumulating Pi connection after `/reload`, or establish the claimed single Pi music-client lifecycle. Yet the plan makes all of those acceptance claims. Use an appropriate, stable client/peer inventory (excluding the plan's disposed generation probe), plus Pi process/connection evidence before and after reload and exits, and state exactly which comparisons establish each claim.

### Medium — Verify commands contain prohibited recursive deletion

Both owned-root verification snippets execute `rm -rf -- "$oc_root"` or `rm -rf -- "$pi_root"`. Even with ownership and parent guards, the reviewer brief explicitly requires sane verify commands and calls out `rm -rf` as disallowed. Replace these with a narrowly scoped cleanup mechanism that retains the canonical-path, direct-parent, non-symlink, prefix, and ownership checks without placing `rm -rf` in a verify command.
